Here is an example of code that will scroll to the top of a page and stay there:
```
function scrollToTop() {
  // Get all elements with the class "to-top"
  const toTopElements = document.getElementsByClassName("to-top");

  // Loop through each element and remove the "to-top" class
  for (let i = 0; i < toTopElements.length; i++) {
    toTopElements[i].classList.remove("to-top");
  }

  // Get the top of the page
  const top = window.pageYOffset;

  // Scroll to the top of the page
  window.scrollTo(0, top);

  // Add the "to-top" class to the current element
  document.body.classList.add("to-top");
}
```
To use this code, you will need to add a "to-top" class to the element you want to scroll to and call the `scrollToTop()` function when you want to scroll to the top of the page. For example:
```
<button onclick="scrollToTop()">Scroll to Top</button>
```
You can also use the code to automatically scroll to the top of the page after a certain amount of time has passed. For example:
```
setInterval(scrollToTop, 3000); // Scroll to the top of the page every 3 seconds
```
