Vicuna is a high-performance, distributed key-value store that is designed to be used as a backend for node.js apps. To install Vicuna on a server, you will first need to have a server with Node.js and npm (Node Package Manager) installed. Then, you can install Vicuna by running the following command in your server's terminal:
```
npm install -g vicuna
```
This will install Vicuna globally on your server, and you will be able to use it as a backend for your node.js apps by importing it and using its API in your code.

Here is an example of how you might use Vicuna in a node.js app:
```
const Vicuna = require('vicuna');
const client = new Vicuna({
  host: 'http://localhost:8001',
  keyId: 'my-key-id',
  secretId: 'my-secret-id'
});

client.set('my-key', 'my-value', (err, result) => {
  console.log(result);
});

client.get('my-key', (err, result) => {
  console.log(result);
});
```
This code creates a new client object that is connected to the Vicuna instance running on the server at `http://localhost:8001`. The client is authenticated using the `keyId` and `secretId` properties, which can be obtained by generating them in the Vicuna web UI. The `set` and `get` methods are used to store and retrieve data from the key-value store.

