When using the `@ViewChildren` decorator in Angular, the `ngAfterContentInit` lifecycle hook will be called after the Angular framework has initialized the content projected into the component's template.

This hook is called after Angular projects the content into the component's view, but before the view is rendered to the DOM. It is passed an `element` parameter that contains the projected content, which you can use to access the data that was projected into the component.

Here's an example of how to use the `ngAfterContentInit` hook to access data in a component that uses the `@ViewChildren` decorator:
```typescript
import { Component, ViewChildren, ElementRef } from '@angular/core';

@Component({
  selector: 'app-example',
  template: `
    <ng-container *ngFor="let child of viewChildren.toArray()">{{ child.nativeElement.innerText }}</ng-container>
  `,
})
export class ExampleComponent {
  @ViewChildren('example') examples: ElementRef[];

  ngAfterContentInit() {
    console.log(this.examples[0].nativeElement.innerText);
  }
}
```
In this example, the `ExampleComponent` defines a template that uses the `*ngFor` directive to iterate over the `viewChildren` property, which is an array of references to the projected elements. The `ngAfterContentInit` hook is used to access the data in one of the projected elements after the view has been initialized.

Note that the `ngAfterContentInit` hook is only called for components that use the `@ViewChildren` decorator to project elements into their templates. It is not called for components that use other data binding mechanisms, such as `@Input` or two-way data binding with `class` or `property`.

