Vue 3 introduced a new public API for composing components, called the Composition API. To call a Composition API method outside of a component, you can use the `vm` global object, which is a reference to the Vue instance.

Here is an example:
```php
<template>
  <div>
    <my-component :message="message"></my-component>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
import { ref } from 'vue'

export default {
  data() {
    return {
      message: 'Hello World'
    }
  },
  methods: {
    callCompositionApiMethod() {
      this.message = 'Hello from Composition API'
    }
  }
}
</script>
```
In this example, we have a component that uses a ref to store a message. We also have a method called `callCompositionApiMethod` that updates the message. To call this method from outside the component, we can use the `vm` global object:
```php
<template>
  <div>
    <my-component :message="message"></my-component>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
import { ref } from 'vue'
import { MyComponent } from './MyComponent.vue'

export default {
  data() {
    return {
      message: 'Hello World'
    }
  },
  components: {
    MyComponent
  }
  methods: {
    callCompositionApiMethod() {
      const vm = this.$root
      vm.callCompositionApiMethod()
    }
  }
}
</script>
```
In this example, we have a root component that has a ref to a message and a method called `callCompositionApiMethod`. We can access this method from outside the component using the `vm` global object.

Note that you need to make sure that you only call Composition API methods that are intended to be used outside the component, and not any methods that are intended to be used inside the component.

