To create a cache of nodes in Typescript using an array and automatic timeout, you can use the `` Map `` data structure and the `` setTimeout `` function. Here's an example:


```typescript
import { Map, Set, SetTimeout } from 'typescript';

const cache = new Map<Node, Node>();

const addToCache = (node: Node): Node => {
  const cachedNode = cache.get(node);
  if (cachedNode) {
    return cachedNode;
  }

  const newNode = new Node(node.id, node.value, node.expireTime);
  cache.set(node, newNode);

  setTimeout(() => {
    if (cache.size > 10) {
      const nodeToRemove = cache.keys().next().value;
      cache.delete(nodeToRemove);
    }
  }, node.expireTime);

  return newNode;
};

```

In this example, we're using the `` Map `` data structure to store the nodes in the cache. The `` addToCache `` function takes a node as input and returns a new node that is stored in the cache. The `` setTimeout `` function is used to remove nodes from the cache when their expire time has passed. The `` Set `` data structure is used to remove nodes from the cache when their expire time has passed.

You can use this `` addToCache `` function with the `` cache `` map like this:


```typescript
const node1 = new Node('1', 'value1', 1000);
const node2 = new Node('2', 'value2', 2000);

const cachedNode1 = cache.get(node1);
const cachedNode2 = cache.get(node2);

const node1Cache = addToCache(node1);
const node2Cache = addToCache(node2);

console.log(cache.size); // 2
